  users              id, name, email, created_at
  tasks              id, title, description, status,
                     creator_id, assignee_id, created_at, updated_at
  comments           id, task_id, author_id, body, created_at

  subscriptions      user_id, task_id, state, reason, created_at
                     PK (user_id, task_id)
                     state:  'subscribed' | 'muted'
                     reason: 'creator' | 'assignee' | 'commenter'
                             | 'mentioned' | 'manual'

  events             id, task_id, actor_id, type, payload, created_at
                     type: 'task.assigned' | 'comment.created'
                     append-only, never updated or deleted

  notifications      id, user_id, event_id, read_at, created_at
                     UNIQUE (user_id, event_id)

  [1mThree things that table layout buys:[0m

  [3mMuting works properly.[0m [38;5;153mstate = 'muted'[39m is a real row, not an absent one. Absence means "no relationship yet,
  auto-subscribe when one forms"; muted means "I have decided, don't re-subscribe me." Without the distinction, getting
  assigned a task you muted silently re-subscribes you, which is the single most annoying bug in this category of
  feature.

  [3mA mention and a subscription produce one notification, not two.[0m A comment emits exactly one [38;5;153mcomment.created[39m event.
  Fan-out computes recipients as [38;5;153msubscribers ∪ mentioned − actor[39m, and the [38;5;153mUNIQUE (user_id, event_id)[39m constraint makes
  the dedup an invariant the database enforces rather than something the code remembers to do. Whether you were
  mentioned is a flag in the event payload, so the UI can render "Sam mentioned you" instead of "Sam commented" off the
  same row.

  [3mEmail stays additive.[0m [38;5;153mnotifications[39m has no in-app-specific column — [38;5;153mread_at[39m is inbox state, not delivery state. Adding
  email later is a new consumer reading [38;5;153mevents[39m or [38;5;153mnotifications[39m, plus its own delivery table. It doesn't touch the
  write path.


